messages.body_plain:
I would like to use a hierarchical model to jointly estimate
parameters for about 200 groups, each with about 30 datapoints.  I've
tried a number of formulations (using fewer parameters that vary by
group, using normal distributions rather something more dispersed,
having more or fewer priors and hyperpriors), but I cannot get Stan
(I'm using rstan) to produce a result in fewer than about 20 hours
(and my preferred formulation would take much longer).

Below is my code (one of the "preferred" versions).  Do you have any
suggestions for speeding this up by an order of magnitude or two?  The
number of datapoints is not consistent across the groups.

  data {
    int<lower=0> N; // datapoints
    int<lower=0> M; // groups
    // the data
    real yy[N];
    real<lower=0> xx[N];
    real zz1[N];
    real zz2[N];
    // group identifier
    int<lower=0> group[N];
  }
  parameters {
    // parameter of interest
    real beta;

    // group parameters
    real alpha[M];
    real gamma1[M];
    real gamma2[M];
    real<lower=0> sigma2[M];
    real<lower=1> nu;

    // hyperparameters
    real mu_alpha;
    real<lower=0> sigma2_alpha;
    real mu_gamma[2];
    real<lower=0> sigma2_gamma[2];
    real<lower=0> nu_sigma2;
    real<lower=0> s_sigma2;
  }
  model {
    // priors
    alpha ~ normal(mu_alpha, sigma2_alpha);
    gamma1 ~ normal(mu_gamma[1], sigma2_gamma[1]);
    gamma2 ~ normal(mu_gamma[2], sigma2_gamma[2]);
    for (m in 1:M)
      sigma2[m] ~ scaled_inv_chi_square(nu_sigma2, s_sigma2);
    1 / nu ~ uniform(0, 1);

    // core model
    for (n in 1:N)
      yy[n] ~ student_t(nu, alpha[group[n]] + beta * xx[n] +
gamma1[group[n]] * zz1[n] + gamma2[group[n]] * zz2[n],
sigma2[group[n]]);
  }

Thanks for any suggestions!

 - James

On Tuesday, April 30, 2013 10:00:46 PM UTC-4, jrising wrote:

> Below is my code (one of the "preferred" versions).  Do you have any
> suggestions for speeding this up by an order of magnitude or two?
>

>   model {
>     // priors
>     alpha ~ normal(mu_alpha, sigma2_alpha);
>     gamma1 ~ normal(mu_gamma[1], sigma2_gamma[1]);
>     gamma2 ~ normal(mu_gamma[2], sigma2_gamma[2]);
>     for (m in 1:M)
>       sigma2[m] ~ scaled_inv_chi_square(nu_sigma2, s_sigma2);

This won't make a huge difference but I think you can just do (without an explicit loop)

sigma2 ~ scaled_inv_chi_square(nu_sigma2, s_sigma2);

>     1 / nu ~ uniform(0, 1);

Here you need to manually adjust lp__ for the change-of-variables.

>     // core model
>     for (n in 1:N)
>       yy[n] ~ student_t(nu, alpha[group[n]] + beta * xx[n] +
>         gamma1[group[n]] * zz1[n] + gamma2[group[n]] * zz2[n],
>         sigma2[group[n]]);
>   }

This is probably the only place where you can get a appreciable speedup. If you declare a vector[N] called location and another vector[N] called scale at the top of the model block and do

for(n in 1:N) {
  location[n] <- alpha[group[n]] + beta * xx[n] + gamma1[group[n]] * zz1[n] + gamma2[group[n]] * zz2[n];
  scale[n] <- sigma2[group[n]];
}
y ~ student_t(nu, location, scale);

it will go faster.

Ben

Hi Bob,

I'm following up on an old conversation you posted on the development mailing list, regarding saving log likelihoods for WAIC.

>From your post I understand that ll_y should save the log-likelihoods for each data point for each sample:

generated quantities {
     double ll_y[N,T];

     for (n in 1:N)
       for (t in 1:T)
         ll_y[N,T] <- normal_log(alpha[n] + beta[n] * (x[t] - xbar),sigma_y);
   }

Shouldn't it be:

ll_y[n,t] <- normal_log(alpha[n] + beta[n] * (x[t] - xbar),sigma_y);

instead of:

ll_y[N,T] <- normal_log(alpha[n] + beta[n] * (x[t] - xbar),sigma_y);

I'm asking because I tried something equivalent and the extracted ll_y array was all 0's whereas using the ll_y[N,T] version yet wasn't when I used the ll_y[n,t] version.

Does this make sense?

Thanks,

rubi

Dear Stan List,

I am relatively new to Stan, and I'd like to implement the following model:

i=1...n
Y[i,1] ~ poisson(lam[i,1])
Y[i,2] ~ poisson(lam[i,2])
lam[,] = exp(mu[,] + b[,])
b[i,1:2] ~ multi_normal(c(0,0), Sigma[1:2,1:2])
Sigma[1,1] <- sig1*sig1
Sigma[2,2] <- sig2*sig2
Sigma[1,2] <- sig1*sig2*tau
Sigma[2,1] <- Sigma[1,2]

Y is observed, sig1, sig2, mu are known, and tau is the unknown correlation parameter.

This is the R code to simulate the data:

library(MASS)
set.seed(1234)
n <- 100
beta1 <- c(1, -1)
beta2 <- c(0.5, 0.5)
sigma1 <- 0.5
sigma2 <- 0.8
tau12 <- 0.5
x <- runif(n)
X <- model.matrix(~x)
mu1 <- drop(X %*% beta1)
mu2 <- drop(X %*% beta2)
Sig <- diag(c(sigma1, sigma2)^2)
Sig[2,1] <- Sig[1,2] <- sigma1*sigma2*tau12
b <- mvrnorm(n, c(0,0), Sig)
Y1 <- rpois(n, exp(mu1 + b[,1]))
Y2 <- rpois(n, exp(mu2 + b[,2]))

dat <- list(n = n, Y=cbind(Y1,Y2), mu=cbind(mu1,mu2),
    sigma1=sigma1, sigma2=sigma2)

I tried to adapt pieces from the Stan manual and birats example, with no success so far. Here is the best I could come up with:

data {
    int<lower=0> n;
    int Y[n,2];
    row_vector[n] mu;
    real<lower=0> sigma1;
    real<lower=0> sigma2;
}
parameters {
    real<lower=-1,upper=1> tau;
    row_vector[n] loglam;
}
transformed parameters {
    cov_matrix[2] Sigma;
    Sigma[1,1] <- sigma1*sigma1;
    Sigma[2,2] <- sigma2*sigma2;
    Sigma[1,2] <- sigma1*sigma2*tau;
    Sigma[2,1] <- sigma1*sigma2*tau;
}
model {
    for (i in 1:n) {
        loglam[i] ~ multi_normal(mu[i], Sigma);
        Y[i] ~ poisson(exp(loglam[i]));
    }
}

but I get this error message:

TRANSLATING MODEL 'model' FROM Stan CODE TO C++ CODE NOW.
Error in stanc(file = file, model_code = model_code, model_name = model_name,  :
  failed to parse Stan model 'model' with error message:
EXPECTATION FAILURE LOCATION: file=input; line=22, column=9

        loglam[i] ~ multi_normal(mu[i], Sigma);
        ^-- here

DIAGNOSTIC(S) FROM PARSER:
no matches for function name="multi_normal_log"
    arg 0 type=real
    arg 1 type=real
    arg 2 type=matrix
available function signatures for multi_normal_log:
0.  multi_normal_log(vector, vector, matrix) : real
unknown distribution=multi_normal
Parser expecting: "}"

Any help would be much appreciated!

Thanks,

Peter Solymos

messages.body_snippet:
What you want is

model {
   loglam ~ multi_normal(mu,Sigma);
   Y ~ poisson(exp(loglam));
}

You may also need to define Y, mu and loglam as column vectors instead
of row vectors.

Cheers,
Marcus

On

